  "final_text": "**No, not as it is.** The certificate in your files has three problems for that claim.\n\n1. **It has expired.** It was valid until 31.08.2026. The renewal inspection is booked for 22.10.2026, but there's no new certificate in the files. So right now you have no valid certificate to point to. (You said today is 16 October; my system date is 26 October. Either way the certificate has lapsed, and if the inspection has already happened, the new certificate isn't here yet.)\n\n2. **It covers only 12 of your 40 ha (30%).** The certificate says the other 28 ha are not certified. \"Certified organic vineyards\" next to *all* your wines tells customers that every wine comes from certified vineyards. That's untrue for most of your 550,000 bottles.\n\n3. **It covers only the vineyard, not the cellar.** \"Cellar and winemaking: not certified.\" So even a wine made only from the 12 certified hectares is not a certified organic wine. In the EU, which is 74% of your sales (Germany, Sweden, Poland), organic wine is regulated as a product, and winemaking has its own organic rules. Putting an organic reference next to a wine that isn't certified, including wording like \"from organic vineyards,\" is likely to be treated as misleading. Retailers and importers like Nordvik Vin AB usually check this.\n\n**What you could do instead:**\n- **Now:** Leave the line off the shelf cards.\n- **After renewal**, if you want to mention it, use an accurate company-level statement that isn't placed next to specific wines. For example: \"12 of our 40 ha of vineyards are certified organic (control body XX-BIO-000, certificate valid until …).\" Ask Biosertifika Kontrola, and the importer for each market, whether even that wording is allowed in their market.\n- **If you want organic wines:** Get the cellar and winemaking certified too. Then put the organic claim only on wines made entirely from the certified 12 ha, with the control body code and logo the rules require.\n\nThis is based on the two files and general EU organic labelling rules, not legal advice. Your control body has the final say on the exact wording.",
